fe Sciences at Imperial College London invites applications for fully funded PhD studentships commencing 2 October 2027.\nWe are offering projects across a wide range of Life Science disciplines, from molecular structures to global ecology and all scales in between.\nAvailable research areas and supervisors\nA list of available supervisors can be found here, and an overview of research themes in the Department is also available Research Themes\nProspective applicants are encouraged to contact supervisors directly for informal discussions and project-specific information before submitting a formal application.\nEligibility\nApplicants must hold (or expect to obtain) at least an Upper Second Class Honours (2:1) degree in a biological or related science.\nCandidates with a master\u2019s degree in addition to a BSc may be given preference.\nMust meet the eligibility requirements for home fee status, for example as a UK national or through an eligible UK settlement scheme. Please refer to https://www.ukcisa.org.uk/student-advice/find-your-fee-status/ for further information.\nUnfortunately, international students are not eligible for this call.\nFunding\nThe studentships will cover UK tuition fees and will provide an annual tax-free maintenance stipend at the standard UKRI level, currently at \u00a323,805 (rising annually by an amount linked to inflation), in 12 monthly instalments. Studentships are expected to last for 36 months, subject to satisfactory progress.\nHow to apply\nContact supervisors from the eligibility list to discuss potential projects.\nApplications must be submitted in one sitting, use this 2026 Application-Preview.pdf to help you draft your application. AI must not be used when writing personal statements, supporting statements and CVs.\nApplications should be submitted our application page\nApplication deadline: 08/01/2027\nFor more information or general support please contact dtp@imperial.ac.uk\nImportant further information\nAs part of this studentship call, the Department is also offering a minimum of two Widening Participation (WP) PhD Studentships designed to broaden access to doctoral study. These studentships offer the same funding and opportunities as the main competition.\nApplicants may choose to be considered for both the Standard and Widening Participation (WP) studentship schemes. Only one application form should be submitted, but you will be asked to indicate on the form whether you wish to be considered for one or both schemes.\nFurther details about the WP studentships can be found here.\nEnquiries\nResearch\nPostgraduate Research & Doctoral Training Programme Administrator\nJames Ferguson\njames.ferguson@imperial.ac.uk\nDoctoral Training Programme Administrator\nRozan Hamilton-Nixon\nr.hamilton-nixon@imperial.ac.uk\nMasters\nHead of Masters\nProfessor James Rosindell\nj.rosindell@imperial.ac.uk\npgtlifesciences@imperial.ac.uk (South Kensington courses)\npgt.silwood@imperial.ac.uk (Silwood Park courses)\nOther contacts\nDirector of Postgraduate Studies\nDr Tony Southall\nt.southall@imperial.ac.uk\nPostgraduate Senior Tutors (South Kensington)\nDr Abigail Clements\na.clements@imperial.ac.uk\nDr Marco Trizzino\nm.trizzino@imperial.ac.uk\nPostgraduate Senior Tutors (Silwood Park)\nDr Emma Ransome\ne.ransome@imperial.ac.uk\nDr Julia Schroeder\njulia.schroeder@imperial.ac.uk\nTerms and conditions for Imperial students\nCompetence Standards for MSc & MRes Programmes [PDF, 70Kb]\nProgramme specifications\nContact and Links\nDepartment of Life Sciences\nSouth Kensington Campus\nExhibition Road\nLondon\nSW7 2AZ\nSouth Kensington Campus map\nSilwood Park Campus\nBuckhurst Road\nAscot, Berkshire\nSL5 7PY\nSilwood Park Campus map\nFind us on social media\nYouTube\nInstagram\nBlueSky\nUseful Links\nPopular links\nCanvas\nContact the Service Desk\nJobs\nLibrary services\nOutlook email online\nFaculties\nEngineering\nMedicine\nNatural Sciences\nImperial Business School\nDirectories\nAdmin and support service